// Russian railway stations from trolleway/russian-railways-simplegeodata (stations.gpkg).
// Dedup strategy (drop, in order):
//  1. English OR Russian name matches a curated RU station (after normalising
//     case/punctuation/ё→е) — Ekaterinburg=Yekaterinburg, Moskva=Moscow,
//     Tinda=Tynda, Izevsk=Izhevsk, Taishet=Tayshet, Oryol=Orel, ...
//  2. within 3 km of a curated hub — Adler=Sochi, SPb=St P, Tula-1=Tula,
//     Yaroslavl-Moskovsky, Tomsk-2, Tikhookeanskaya=Nakhodka, ...
//
// Connectivity:
//   (a) every spur always connects to its nearest curated RU hub by great-
//       circle — preserves long-distance fan-in so Moscow/Murmansk can still
//       reach the leaves;
//   (b) plus up to 2 nearest pool-wide neighbours (curated or other spur)
//       within 250 km — builds intra-corridor chains so the Kola Line spurs
//       (Olenegorsk → Apatity → Kandalaksha → Loukhi → Belomorsk → Petrozavodsk)
//       link to each other, not all radially back to Murmansk.
// Edge keys are sorted endpoints so A→B + B→A never both appear.
// Replaces the old 'single nearest hub' rule which left contour plateaus —
// from Murmansk nothing reachable between 6 h and 18 h, because every Kola
// Line station radiated to Murmansk rather than chaining south.

